  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 217 AM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain showers, with the possibility of a few \nmountain snow showers, will continue this afternoon and again on\nSunday with the areal coverage tapering off throughout the \nweekend. The lack of any appreciable cold air will limit any \nsnow to the highest of summits with rainfall being the most \nlikely precipitation type. On Monday, another low pressure \nsystem will track across our forecast area and will bring \nanother slug of cold rain to the region. Models are in very good\nagreement that 850mb and 925mb temperatures will be well above \nfreezing so even the highest of summit will be stuck with a cold\nrain. This rainfall will be extremely beneficial as we try to \nalleviate some of the developing drought concerns with under a \nhalf of an inch of rainfall currently forecasted.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Precipitation chances wind down Monday night \nwith atmospheric profiles cooling. Although it will be cold \nenough to transition rain to snow, the bulk of the moisture is \nexpected to have exited the region with only higher terrain and \nnortheastern Vermont potentially picking up token amounts of \nsnowfall. Tuesday through the end of the week, temperatures are \nstrongly favored to warm well above seasonal averages, nearly 10\ndegrees by Thursday and Friday in the upper 40s to low 50s. The\nprojected flow pattern continues to be zonal promoting fast \nmoving waves. The next such wave is expected late in the week, \nas early as Thursday, but models are split on timing. Some \ndevelop a lee side low off the Rocky Mountains and push it \nquickly eastward in zonal flow by Thursday while others deepen \nthe low further resulting in a later approach more Friday or \nover the next weekend. Either way, it looks like this system \nwould start on the warmer side resulting in rainfall at least at\nthe onset.",
